    x+y=\\frac{2(t-1)}{t-2}.\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(t\\ge d+2\\ge 3\\), the slope of \\(S_{t}\\) equals  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{t}= -\\frac{t-2}{t-2}= -1+\\frac{2}{t-2},\n     \\]\n\n     which is **different** from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n     Moreover, for any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     a+b=t\\Longrightarrow (a,b)=\\bigl(1+(a-1),\\,1+(b-1)\\bigr)\n     \\]\n\n     and the vector \\((a-1,b-1)\\) is a scalar multiple of \\((t-2,t-2)\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\), i.e. on \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n     Thus the \\(k=n-d\\) sunny lines \\(S_{t}\\;(t=d+2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) cover precisely the remaining points, namely those with \\(a+b\\ge d+2\\).\n\n   * **Verification.**  \n     Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a diagonal line \\(D_{c}\\) (if \\(a+b\\le d+1\\)) or to a sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) (if \\(a+b\\ge d+2\\)). Hence all required points are covered.  \n     The total number of lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     d+(n-d)=n,\n     \\]\n\n     and exactly \\(k=n-d\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Because the choice \\(h=v=0,\\;d=n-k\\) works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for arbitrary \\(k\\) yields the desired classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by diagonal lines).\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare not sunny and they cover \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(c:=a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=c\\) and lies on \\(L_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line containing all points of a given diagonal).\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le n+1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\;(t-2)x+(t-2)y=2(t-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n1. \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny;  \n2. every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe two distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(1,t-1),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t-1,1)\n\\]\n\nboth satisfy (1), so (1) is the equation of the line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{t}= -\\frac{t-2}{t-2}= -1+\\frac{2}{t-2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) because \\(t\\ge 3\\); thus \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) with \\(a+b=t\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(1+(a-1),\\;1+(b-1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a-1+b-1=t-2\\), the vector \\((a-1,b-1)\\) is a scalar multiple of \\((t-2,t-2)\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\(P_{t}\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\), i.e. on \\(S_{t}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be given.  Put  \n\n\\[\nd:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }0\\le d\\le n).\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(d\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,d+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,d+1\\}\\).\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{d+2,d+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) by (1).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2, each \\(S_{t}\\) contains **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\).\n\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-(d+2)+1 = n-d = k\n\\]\n\nsuch lines; they are distinct because they have different constants \\(t\\).\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 verification.**  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b\\le d+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\) from (2).  \n* If \\(a+b\\ge d+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a+b}\\) from Step\u202f2.\n\nHence all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.  \nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\nd + (n-d)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k=n-d\\) of them are sunny (the lines \\(S_{t}\\)).  All lines are distinct because the families \\(\\{D_{c}\\}\\) and \\(\\{S_{t}\\}\\) have different slopes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* For \\(k=0\\) the construction of Lemma\u202f1 gives a family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\).  \n* For any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above provides a family of \\(n\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; }.\n\\]"
