"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for \\(n=3\\) only \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) can occur, whereas for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Notation.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nThe proof consists of three parts.\n\n1. **Existence.**  \n   - *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n     \\[\n     L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     are all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n   - *\\(k=1\\).*  Replace the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) in (1) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n     The line \\(y=x\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and all points \\((i,i)\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\); the remaining points are still covered by the diagonals (1).  \n   - *\\(k=3\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \n     Use the vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3)\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     (these are non\u2011sunny).  \n     After (2) the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     U_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     which consists of the six points  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     S_1&:\\;(n-2,1)\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n     S_2&:\\;(n-2,2)\\;(n,1),\\\\\n     S_3&:\\;(n-2,3)\\;(n-1,1)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     have slopes \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{\\,n-1-(n-2)}=1,\\;\n     \\frac{1-2}{\\,n-(n-2)}=-\\frac12,\\;\n     \\frac{1-3}{\\,n-1-(n-2)}=-2\\), respectively; all three are sunny and together they cover the six points (3).  \n     Hence we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The two smallest diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2:\\;x+y=2\\;(=\\{(1,1)\\}),\\qquad   \n   D_3:\\;x+y=3\\;(=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line containing two points of \\(D_3\\) is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \n   If a covering contains only two sunny lines, then at least one of the lines \\(D_2\\) or \\(D_3\\) must be omitted from the set of non\u2011sunny lines (otherwise all points would already be covered by non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   Omission of \\(D_2\\) forces the point \\((1,1)\\) to be covered by a sunny line; omission of \\(D_3\\) forces the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) to be covered by sunny lines, but a single sunny line cannot contain both of them.  \n   Consequently at least three sunny lines are necessary, i.e. \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Assume a covering with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   After (6) the uncovered points are exactly the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U'_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains the six points listed in (3) when \\(k=3\\) and, for \\(k\\ge4\\), contains the ten points\n\n   \\[\n   (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4),\\;\n   (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;\n   (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;\n   (n,1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The configuration (8) is precisely the set \\(T_4\\) (a triangle of side\u2011length\u202f4).  \n   A sunny line can intersect each diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) at most once, and in \\(T_4\\) every diagonal contains at most two points; consequently a sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(T_4\\).  \n   With \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(2k\\le8\\) points of \\(T_4\\), but \\(T_4\\) has ten points \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Hence no covering with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nCombining the three parts yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{slope}(\\ell)\\) its slope (possibly \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\nA line of the form \\(x=c\\) (vertical) has slope \\(\\infty\\); a line \\(y=c\\) (horizontal) has slope \\(0\\); a line \\(x+y=c\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \nAll three families are non\u2011sunny; any other line is sunny.\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\): a line not parallel to a diagonal meets each of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(a+b=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) in at most one point, while a diagonal itself contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  Existence results  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  (\\(k=0\\)).  \nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  (\\(k=1\\)).  \nReplace in (11) the line \\(L_2\\;(x+y=2)\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and also all points \\((i,i)\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\bigr\\rfloor\\); all other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the remaining diagonals \\(L_c\\;(c\\ge3)\\).  \nHence a covering with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### Lemma 2.3  (\\(k=3\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \nTake the vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3)\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(which are distinct because \\(n-3\\ge1\\)).  \nAll points with abscissa \\(a\\le n-3\\) are covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely the six points\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1&=(n-2,1), & P_2&=(n-2,2), & P_3&=(n-2,3),\\\\\nP_4&=(n-1,1), & P_5&=(n-1,2), & P_6&=(n,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1&:\\;P_1P_5\\quad\\bigl(\\operatorname{slope}=1\\bigr),\\\\\nS_2&:\\;P_2P_6\\quad\\bigl(\\operatorname{slope}=-\\tfrac12\\bigr),\\\\\nS_3&:\\;P_3P_4\\quad\\bigl(\\operatorname{slope}=-2\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny.  \nEach of the six points (14) lies on exactly one of the lines (15), and the lines are distinct.  \nConsequently the family consisting of the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines (13) together with the three sunny lines (15) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly three sunny lines.  \nThus \\(k=3\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a covering of \\(T_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nConsider the two smallest diagonals\n\n\\[\nD_2:\\;x+y=2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad   \nD_3:\\;x+y=3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nIf both \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\) belong to the