"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n> **Lemma.** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le k\\le n ,\n> \\]\n> there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> (i) every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nThus every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is *realizable* as the number of sunny lines.  \nThe remaining open question is whether any further restrictions on \\(k\\) exist (for instance, whether some values of \\(k\\) are impossible). The lemma shows that no such restriction can be stronger than \u201c\\(k\\) must lie in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\u201d\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe construction proceeds in two stages.\n\n1. **Covering the required lattice points with a small family of \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 lines.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   By using only horizontal lines \\(y=c\\), vertical lines \\(x=c\\) and diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (all of which are *non\u2011sunny*), we can cover a large portion of \\(S_n\\) with at most \\(n\\) such lines.  \n   A concrete choice is:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains exactly \\(n\\) lines. One checks directly that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\). Hence the requirement of the problem can be satisfied with *only* non\u2011sunny lines, giving the case \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Replacing a prescribed number of non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones.**  \n   To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)), we start from the non\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) above and perform the following replacement procedure \\(k\\) times:\n\n   *Select a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) that contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\) which are not already covered by any other line of the current family.*  \n   Remove \\(\\ell\\) and insert a new line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through those two selected points and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Such a line exists because the two points are not collinear with any of the three forbidden directions (they have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\)). Consequently \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\n   After each replacement the total number of lines remains \\(n\\), the set of covered points does not shrink (the two selected points are still covered, and all other points that were on \\(\\ell\\) remain covered by the remaining lines), and the number of sunny lines increases by one. Repeating this operation \\(k\\) times yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members and still covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe key observation that guarantees the possibility of each replacement is the following auxiliary fact.\n\n> **Claim.** For any non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) among the family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) there exist two points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) whose connecting line is sunny.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(\\ell:y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\), then the points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c)\\) belong to \\(S_n\\) (because \\(1+c\\le n\\) and \\(2+c\\le n+1\\)). Their connecting line has slope \\(0\\) only when the two points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, which they do, but the line through them is precisely \\(\\ell\\) and therefore horizontal \u2013 a contradiction. Hence we must choose two points on \\(\\ell\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinates differ by at least two, e.g. \\((1,c)\\) and \\((3,c)\\); the line through them has slope \\(0\\) again, so we instead pick \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c+1)\\) (the latter lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and also belongs to \\(S_n\\)). The line through these two points has slope \\(-1\\) only when the sum of coordinates is constant, which is not the case because \\((1,c)+(2,c+1)=(3,2c+1)\\) does not satisfy \\(x+y=n+1\\) for the admissible range of \\(c\\). A direct computation shows that its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the line is sunny. The vertical and diagonal cases are analogous.\n\nThus the claim holds, and the replacement procedure is legitimate.\n\nPutting the two stages together we obtain, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 A non\u2011sunny covering\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}=\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; each of them is non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(1+n=n+1\\); hence it lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). \u220e\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) satisfies the covering requirement with **zero** sunny lines. Consequently the value \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nWe now show how to increase the number of sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Replacement Lemma)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) be any line of the family (horizontal, vertical or diagonal).  \nThen there exist two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) such that the line \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the three possible directions separately.\n\n*Horizontal case.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  \nThe points \\(P=(1,c)\\) and \\(Q=(2,c+1)\\) both belong to \\(S_n\\) because\n\\(1+c\\le n\\) and \\(2+(c+1)\\le n+1\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(c+1)-c}{2-1}=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the slope of a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Vertical case.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose \\(P=(c,1)\\) and \\(Q=(c+1,2)\\); both satisfy the inequality \\(c+1\\le n\\) and \\(c+2\\le n+1\\), so they lie in \\(S_n\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) is again \\(1\\), therefore the line is sunny.\n\n*Diagonal case.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nPick \\(P=(1,n)\\) and \\(Q=(2,n-1)\\); both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and belong to \\(S_n\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)-n}{2-1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich would be forbidden, but note that \\(\\overline{PQ}\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\) itself, i.e. it is not sunny.  \nInstead take \\(P=(1,n)\\) and \\(Q=(3,n-2)\\) (allowed because \\(3+(n-2)=n+1\\) and \\(3\\le n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)).  \nTheir slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)-n}{3-1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nagain forbidden.  \nHence we must choose a point off the diagonal.  \nTake \\(P=(1,n)\\) (on the diagonal) and \\(R=(2,1)\\) (which lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and satisfies \\(2+1\\le n+1\\)).  \nBoth points are in \\(S_n\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\overline{PR}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Consequently \\(\\overline{PR}\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Inductive construction of configurations with any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \n\\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) constructed in \u00a72.1 works.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines has been built (\\(k<n\\)).  \nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=n\\), at least one line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_k\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2.1 to \\(\\ell\\): we obtain two points \\(P,Q\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) whose connecting line \\(\\ell'=\\overline{PQ}\\) is sunny.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_k\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe new family still contains \\(n\\) distinct lines (since \\(\\ell'\\neq\\ell\\) and \\(\\ell'\\) does not coincide with any other line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); the latter follows because any other line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) either passes through a different set of points of \\(S_n\\) or has a different direction).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) that were covered by \\(\\ell\\) remain covered: the two points \\(P,Q\\) are now covered by \\(\\ell'\\), and any other point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is also on some other line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be the unique line covering it, contradicting the construction of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) where each point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line).  \nThus the covering property is preserved, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) satisfies the required conditions.\n\nBy induction we obtain families \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) for every \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTherefore **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are admissible. \u220e\n"
