w sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell'_{1}&:y=x-m,\\\\[1mm]\n\\ell'_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n*The line \\(\\ell'_{1}\\).*  \nIts equation is \\(y=x-m\\); it contains all points of \\(A_1\\) except the two extremal points \\((1,2m-1)\\) and \\((2m-1,1)\\).  Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(a+b=2m\\) and therefore \\(b=2m-a\\); substituting into \\(y=x-m\\) gives \\(2m-a=a-m\\), i.e. \\(a=m\\).  Hence the only points of \\(A_1\\) lying on \\(\\ell'_{1}\\) are \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\), which have already been removed from \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell'_{1}\\) covers the whole antidiagonal \\(A_1\\) except the two extremal points.\n\n*The line \\(\\ell'_{2}\\).*  \nIts equation can be written as \\(2y=-x+2m+1\\).  For a point \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(a+b=2m+1\\), i.e. \\(b=2m+1-a\\).  Substituting into the equation of \\(\\ell'_{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2(2m+1-a)=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow a=1\\ \\text{or}\\ a=2m-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell'_{2}\\) contains exactly the two extremal points \\((1,2m)\\) and \\((2m,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) with \\(a\\neq1,2m\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n2b=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow b=\\frac{2m+1-a}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer precisely when \\(a\\) is odd.  Hence all points of \\(A_2\\) with odd abscissa lie on \\(\\ell'_{2}\\).  The remaining points of \\(A_2\\) (those with even abscissa) are exactly the points \\((2,2m-1),(4,2m-3),\\dots ,(2m-2,3)\\); each of them belongs to the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) that we removed, and therefore is already covered by the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_{1},\\ell'_{2}\\}\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \