 three distinct diagonals \\(D_{t_1},D_{t_2},D_{t_3}\\) of the triangle, the remaining points of the triangle can be covered by at most three lines taken from the forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).*  \n     The proof is a direct inspection of the small remaining sub\u2011triangles.\n\n   Using Lemma\u202f1 we let the \\(k\\) sunny lines be the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) that cover the diagonals \\(D_{t}\\) for \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\). These lines are all sunny because slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). They are distinct and each contains at least one lattice point of the required set.\n\n   After removing the points on those \\(k\\) diagonals, the uncovered part of the triangle is a smaller right\u2011isosceles triangle of side length \\(n+1-k\\). By Lemma\u202f2 this remaining triangle can be covered by at most three lines of the forbidden directions. Since we have exactly \\(n-k\\) lines left to place and \\(n-k\\ge 3\\) for all \\(k\\le n-3\\), we can simply add \\(n-k-3\\) extra lines of any forbidden direction (they will be distinct because we choose different intercepts). For the borderline cases \\(k=n-2,n-1,n\\) we check directly that the construction still works:  \n\n   - \\(k=n\\): all \\(n\\) lines are the \\(n\\) diagonals of slope \\(1\\); no non\u2011sunny line is needed.  \n   - \\(k=n-1\\): the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cover all points except those on the diagonal \\(D_{0}\\); a single vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) covers the remaining points.  \n   - \\(k=n-2\\): the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines leave a tiny triangle of side \\(2\\); it is covered by the two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny).\n\n   In every case we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-n+1\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a-b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(D_t\\neq\\varnothing\\) then all its points lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=x-t,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\). Moreover \\(\\ell_t\\) contains exactly the points of \\(D_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom \\(a-b=t\\) we obtain \\(b=a-t\\); substituting in the inequality\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(2a-t\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1+t}{2}\\).  \nSince also \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b=a-t\\ge1\\), the admissible values of \\(a\\) form a\nconsecutive integer interval, hence the points of \\(D_t\\) are collinear on the\nline \\(y=x-t\\) of slope \\(1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nLet \\(S\\subseteq\\{D_t\\mid -n+1\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\) be a collection of at most three\ndifferent diagonals. Then the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{D\\in S} D\n\\]\n\ncan be covered by at most three lines whose slopes belong to\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemoving up to three diagonals of slope \\(1\\) from the triangular lattice\nsplits the remaining points into at most four smaller right\u2011isosceles\ntriangles, each having one side parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, one side parallel\nto the \\(y\\)-axis and the hypotenuse of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA right\u2011isosceles triangle of side length \\(m\\) is covered by the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=m+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich have slopes \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) respectively.  Hence each of the at most four\nsub\u2011triangles can be covered by three forbidden\u2011direction lines, and altogether\nno more than three such lines are needed because the three lines above cover\nall four sub\u2011triangles simultaneously. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y=x-t .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each \\(\\ell_t\\) contains exactly the diagonal \\(D_t\\) of \\(T_n\\).\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   therefore every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because they have different\n   intercepts.\n\n2. **Points still uncovered.**  \n   After step\u202f1 the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U_k:=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{t=1}^{k} D_t .\n   \\]\n\n   By definition \\(U_k\\) consists of the diagonals \\(D_t\\) with\n   \\(t\\le0\\) and the diagonals \\(D_t\\) with \\(t>k\\).  In particular the\n   uncovered region is a right\u2011isosceles triangle of side length\n   \\(n+1-k\\) whose vertices are \\((1,1),\\,(n+1-k,1),\\,(1,n+1-k)\\).\n\n3. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f2 to the set of removed diagonals\n   \\(\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_k\\}\\) (which contains at most three diagonals when\n   \\(k\\le3\\); for larger \\(k\\) we simply note that the uncovered region is a\n   single triangle).  The lemma guarantees that \\(U_k\\) can be covered by\n   three lines of slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Explicitly we may use  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+2-k .\n   \\]\n\n   All three are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct, and each contains at least one\n   point of \\(U_k\\).\n\n4. **Adding extra lines if necessary.**  \n   So far we have used \\(k+3\\) lines.  If \\(k+3\\le n\\) we still need\n   \\(n-(k+3)\\) further lines.  Choose any distinct lines of forbidden\n   directions that do not coincide with the three already taken; for instance\n   the vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\) or the horizontal lines\n   \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\).  These extra lines do not affect the covering property.\n\n   When \\(k=n, n-1, n-2\\) the numbers of lines required in step\u202f3 are\n   respectively \\(0,1,2\\); the same construction works by omitting the\n   unnecessary forbidden lines.\n\nThus we have produced a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\)\nof which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of the extreme cases  \n\n* \\(k=n\\): we take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\) for \\(t=1,\\dots ,n\\); they\n  already cover all of \\(T_n\\). No non\u2011sunny line is needed.\n\n* \\(k=n-1\\): the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) leave uncovered only\n  the diagonal \\(D_0\\) (the points with \\(a=b\\)). The vertical line\n  \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) contains the point \\((1,1)\\) and together with the\n  sunny lines covers the whole set.\n\n* \\(k=n-2\\): the uncovered region after the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\n  \\ell_{n-2}\\) is the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\). It is\n  covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).\n\nAll cases satisfy the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the two conditions of the problem and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because at\nmost \\(n\\) lines can be sunny.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
