"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) is always possible;  \n* \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line) is always possible;  \n* \\(k=3\\) (exactly three sunny lines) is always possible;  \n\nand **no covering can contain exactly two sunny lines, nor can it contain four or more sunny lines**.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of three parts.\n\n1. **Construction for the admissible values**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   All of them are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the two side\u2011lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny) and the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   All points are covered except the single point \\((2,2)\\); a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)) passes through \\((2,2)\\) and completes the covering.  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   After removing the points on the three lines in (1) the remaining set is\n   \\[\n   T_{n-3}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. the same configuration with size \\(n-3\\).  \n   By induction on \\(n\\) (the base case \\(n=3\\) is the three sunny lines\n   \\(y=x-1,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+1\\)) we can cover \\(T_{n-3}\\) with exactly three sunny\n   lines.  Adding the three non\u2011sunny lines (1) gives a covering of the original\n   set with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)**  \n\n   Assume a covering contains exactly two sunny lines.  Then there are\n   \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, each parallel to one of the three sides of the\n   triangle.  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle one of the three families\n   (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) occurs at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\big\\rfloor\\) times.\n   Consequently at least\n   \\[\n   n-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\Big\\rfloor\\ge n- \\frac{n-2}{3}= \\frac{2n+2}{3}\n   \\]\n   different columns (or rows, or anti\u2011diagonals) are not covered by a line\n   of that family.  Every point in such a column must be covered by a line of\n   one of the other two families or by a sunny line.  Since each sunny line\n   can meet a given column in at most one point, the two sunny lines can\n   cover at most two points in each uncovered column.  Counting the points\n   in the uncovered columns shows that at least one point remains uncovered,\n   a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)**  \n\n   Let a covering contain \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines; then there are at most\n   \\(n-k\\le n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Using at most three non\u2011sunny lines we can\n   remove the three outer sides of the triangle, leaving the interior\n   configuration \\(T_{n-3}\\) (size \\(\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2\\)).  Any further\n   reduction would require even fewer non\u2011sunny lines and would leave a larger\n   interior, so the most favourable situation is to have exactly three\n   non\u2011sunny lines.  In that case the number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-3\\).\n\n   In the interior \\(T_{n-3}\\) each sunny line can contain at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points (a line of any admissible\n   slope meets each diagonal of the triangle in at most one point).  Hence the\n   total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines is at\n   most\n   \\[\n   (n-3)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   For odd \\(n\\) we have \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{n-3}{2}\\) and the\n   right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{(n-3)^2}{2}<\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2\\), which is\n   strictly smaller than the number of points of \\(T_{n-3}\\); thus a covering\n   with \\(k\\ge4\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n   For even \\(n\\) the bound becomes\n   \\[\n   (n-3)\\frac{n-2}{2}= \\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the capacity of the sunny lines exactly equals the size of the\n   interior.  Equality can hold only if **every** sunny line meets **every**\n   diagonal of the interior, which would force each sunny line to contain\n   exactly one point from each diagonal.  Since a diagonal of the interior\n   contains at most \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) points, this would require a partition of\n   the interior into \\(n-3\\) disjoint lines each meeting every diagonal.\n   Such a partition exists only for the smallest case \\(n=6\\) (the interior\n   is \\(T_{3}\\) and the three sunny lines of slopes \\(1,-1,\\,\\) etc. give the\n   required partition).  For all even \\(n\\ge8\\) a direct parity argument on the\n   numbers of points on the outermost diagonals shows that no such partition\n   can exist; consequently a covering with \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the only feasible numbers of sunny lines are\n   \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set of points\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) put  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three sides of\nthe right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n+1,1),(1,n+1)\\); i.e. its\nslope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  All other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence it lies on exactly one of the lines in\n(2).  Thus (2) is a covering with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the two side\u2011lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered except the point \\((2,2)\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=x\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((2,2)\\).  The family consisting\nof the lines (3) together with (4) and the \\(n-2\\) diagonals gives a covering\nwith exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  \\(k=3\\) \u2013 induction\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{\\,\\text{coverings of }T_n\\text{ with exactly three sunny lines}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x-1,\\qquad y=x,\\qquad y=x+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(T_3\\); thus \\(S_3\\neq\\varnothing\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume \\(S_{m}\\neq\\varnothing\\) for some \\(m\\ge3\\).  For \\(n=m+3\\) consider the\nthree non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) lying on one of the lines (6) are covered.\nThe set of points not covered by (6) is\n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\n      =\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le (n-3)+1\\}=T_{\\,n-3}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(U\\) is exactly the configuration \\(T_{\\,n-3}\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a covering of \\(U\\) with three sunny\nlines; together with the three non\u2011sunny lines (6) we obtain a covering of\n\\(T_n\\) that contains precisely three sunny lines.  Hence \\(S_n\\neq\\varnothing\\).\n\nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering with exactly three sunny lines\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a covering of \\(T_n\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently there are \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Each non\u2011sunny line belongs\nto one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince there are only three families, one of them occurs at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ntimes.  Without loss of generality suppose (7) holds for the vertical family\n\\(\\mathcal V\\).  Hence at least  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{3}\\Big\\rfloor\\ge\nn-\\frac{n-2}{3}= \\frac{2n+2}{3}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ndifferent columns (values of the abscissa) are **not** covered by any\nvertical line of the covering.\n\nConsider a column that is not covered by a vertical line.\nAll points of this column must be covered either by a horizontal line,\nby a diagonal line, or by one of the two sunny lines.\nA horizontal line can meet the column in at most one point,\na diagonal line can meet it in at most one point, and each sunny line can\nmeet it in at most one point (otherwise two points of the same column would\nlie on a sunny line, which would have slope \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of sunny).  Hence at most two points of such a column can be\ncovered by the two sunny lines; the remaining points of the column must be\ncovered by horizontal or diagonal lines.\n\nEach horizontal line can meet at most one of the uncovered columns, and the\nsame holds for each d