  Hence a covering with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n#### 2.2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n> \u201cTake the two side\u2011lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\qquad y=1\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> and the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n> All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered except the point \\((2,2)\\).  The line  \n> \\[\n> \\ell:\\;y=x\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> has slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((2,2)\\).  The family consisting of the lines (3) together with (4) and the \\(n-2\\) diagonals gives a covering with exactly one sunny line.\u201d\n\n*Critical Error.*  \n1. The diagonal set \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) **includes** the line \\(x+y=4\\), which already contains the point \\((2,2)\\).  Hence the claim \u201call points \u2026 except \\((2,2)\\)\u201d is false.  \n2. Adding the sunny line \\(y=x\\) to the already\u2011\\(n\\) lines (3)\u202f+\u202f\\(n-2\\) diagonals yields **\\(n+1\\) distinct lines**, violating the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines be used.  To obtain a valid covering one would have to delete a diagonal (e.g. \\(x+y=4\\)) and replace it with \\(y=x\\); this step is omitted, so the construction as written does **not** produce a legitimate covering with \\(k=1\\).\n\n#### 2.2.3  \\(k=3\\) \u2013 induction\n> \u201cDefine  \n> \\[\n> S_n:=\\{\\,\\text{coverings of }T_n\\text{ with exactly three sunny lines}\\,\\}.\n> \\]  \n> **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n> \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n> The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x-1,\\qquad y=x,\\qquad y=x+1\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> cover \\(T_3\\); thus \\(S_3\\neq\\varnothing\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error.*  The three lines listed have slope\u202f1 (sunny) but they **miss** the points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\).  Consequently \\(S_3\\) is empty, and the induction cannot start.  The subsequent inductive step, although formally correct assuming a non\u2011empty base case, is therefore irrelevant.\n\n> \u201cInductive step. \u2026 For \\(n=m+3\\)