y all horizontal and diagonal lines is bounded by the number of such lines \u2013 is invalid.  The inequality that follows, leading to the contradiction, rests on this false claim; therefore the impossibility proof for \\(k=2\\) is unsound.\n\n> \u201cSince the total number of horizontal plus diagonal lines is at most \u2026 the total number of points that can be covered \u2026 is at most \u2026 \\(\\frac{6n+2}{3}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) \u2026 a contradiction.\u201d\n\n*Result of Critical Error.*  Because the bound on the covering capacity of horizontal/diagonal lines is based on the false claim, the derived inequality does not follow.  Hence the argument does **not** establish that a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\n> \u201cSuppose a covering contains \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines; then there are at most \\(n-k\\le n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines. \u2026 Using at most three non\u2011sunny lines we can eliminate the three outer sides \u2026 the most favourable situation \u2026 is to have exactly the three lines (9).  \n> After removing (9) the set of still uncovered points is  \n> \\[\n> U=T_{\\,n-3},\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]  \n> which contains \\(\\frac{(n-3)(n-2)}2\\) points.\n\n> \u201cAll remaining lines are sunny. A sunny line can intersect each diagonal of \\(U\\) in at most one point \u2026 Consequently a sunny line contains at most  \n> \\[\n> M:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n> \\tag{11}\n> \\]  \n> points of \\(U\\) (the bound is attained, for example, by a line of slope\u202f1 when \\(n\\) is odd, or by a line of slope \\(-2\\) when \\(n\\) is even).\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The bound \\(M\\) is asserted without proof.  While the claim is plausible (a line of slope\u202f1 meets only the even\u2011indexed diagonals, giving \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\) points), a rigorous argument that **no** sunny line can meet more points of \\(U\\) is missing.\n\n> \u201cIf \\(n\\) is odd \u2