nstruction of Section\u202f2.2.3).\u201d  This discussion appears in the section about \\(k\\ge4\\) and does not address the case \\(k\\ge4\\); it adds confusion rather than supporting the argument.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\n> \u201cThe three constructions of Section\u202f2.2 show that the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable \u2026 The impossibility proofs of Sections\u202f2.3 and\u202f2.4 show that no other non\u2011negative integer can occur. \u2026 the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error.*  The conclusion rests on the faulty constructions for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\) and on the invalid impossibility arguments for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\).  Consequently the statement that the admissible values are precisely \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is **not** established by the presented solution.  \n\n--- \n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution fails to provide correct constructions for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\), and its impossibility arguments for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\) contain critical logical and counting errors.  Moreover, several steps lack the necessary rigorous justification.  Hence the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
