).  \n   The remaining points satisfy either \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   Hence they are covered by the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The lines in (5) are non\u2011sunny, the diagonal is also non\u2011sunny, and together we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  A direct verification shows that every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.*  \n   Let \\(n=2m\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2)\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (all slopes\u202f\\(1\\)).  They cover all points with \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \n   The only points not yet covered are those with \\(a-b=n-1\\), i.e. the single point \\((n,1)\\), and all points with \\(a<b\\).  All points with \\(a<b\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  Adding this line gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny members covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.*  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2)\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   as before.  They miss the points with \\(a-b=n-1\\) (the single point \\((n,1)\\)) and the points with \\(a<b\\).  All points with \\(a<b\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is non\u2011sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Adding this diagonal line yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.*  \n   We prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   \u2013 **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1&:y=x,\\\\\n     \\ell_2&:y=-2x+5,\\\\\n     \\ell_3&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   \u2013 **Inductive step.**  \n     Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n     The set \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by adding the two new antidiagonals  \n\n     \\[\n     D_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m\\},\\qquad   \n     D_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m+1\\}.\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n     (Both consist of \\(2m\\) points.)  \n\n     From \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) we delete the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\); this line contains exactly the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\).  Replace it by the two sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell'_1&:y=x-m,\\\\\n     \\ell'_2&:y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{2m+1}{2},\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n\n     which are respectively of slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (both sunny).  One checks directly that  \n\n     * \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains all points of \\(D_1\\) except \\((1,2m-1)\\) and \\((2m-1,1)\\);  \n     * \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two missing points \\((1,2m-1)\\) and \\((2m-1,1)\\) as well as the whole antidiagonal \\(D_2\\).\n\n     Consequently the new family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{2m+1}\\).  This completes the induction.\n\n   The inductive construction yields, for every odd \\(n\\), a covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (2) with the explicit constructions above we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\) stated at the beginning of the summary. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Preliminaries\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\); a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nFor an integer \\(d\\) we denote  \n\n\\[\nL(d)=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\},\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the line \\(y=x-d\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).  The line \\(y=x-d\\) is sunny for every integer \\(d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nFor a sunny line the slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the line cannot be parallel to any side of the triangle.  Consequently a sunny line meets the boundary of the triangle in at most two points, and the integer points of the line form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\) in the \\(x\\)\u2011direction.  Hence a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  (Indeed, the line of slope\u202f\\(1\\) with equation \\(y=x-d\\) contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, and this number is maximised when \\(|d|=0\\).)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  In each of these three families the line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest row, the longest column or the main diagonal).\n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered by a family with \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nNow we analyse (17).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (17) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk(m+1)+(2m+1-k)(2m+1)\\ge(2m+1)(m+1).\n\\]\n\nAfter simplification this is equivalent to \\(k\\le 2m+1=n\\).  Hence for odd \\(n\\) the inequality imposes no restriction beyond \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (17) becomes  \n\n\\[\nkm+(2m-k)(2m)\\ge 2m(2m+1).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying yields \\(k\\le 2m-1=n-1\\).  Consequently **when \\(n\\) is even one cannot have \\(k=n\\)**.\n\nThus we have proved the necessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[1mm]\nn-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is of slope\u202f\\(1\\) and therefore sunny.  The line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(L(i)\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nConsider the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (20) are horizontal, the line \\(d\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Verification of the covering.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n* If \\(b=n-k\\) then \\(a\\le k+1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Hence \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n* If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n-k+1\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b=n\\).  In the former case \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(d\\); in the latter case \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (19)\u2013(21).  The family consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, and precisely the \\(k\\) lines (19) are sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nAssume \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).  As in the previous section they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\nThe only points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\{(n,1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a<b\\}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(a<b\\) lie on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  Adding \\(v\\) to the family (22) yields a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and the union of the lines is precisely \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nNow let \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Again consider the sunny lines (22) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).  They miss the points described in (23), i.e. the single point \\((n,1)\\) and all points with \\(a<b\\).\n\nAll points with \\(a<b\\) satisfy \\(a+b=n\\); consequently they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nd':\\;x+y=n,\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Adding \\(d'\\) to the family (22) gives a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWe prove by induction on the odd integer \\(n\\) that there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1&:y=x,\\\\\n\\ell_2&:y=-2x+5,\\\\\n\\ell_3&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the point sets  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\\;\n\\{(3,1),(1,2)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich together are exactly the six points of \\(S_3\\).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some odd integer \\(n=2m-1\\;(m\\ge2)\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m-1}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{2m-1}\\}\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nof \\(2m-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{2m-1}\\).\n\nWe shall construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{2m+1}\\) of \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{2m+1}\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by adding the two outer antidiagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_1&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m\\},\\\\\nA_2&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(A_1\\) and \\(A_2\\) contain exactly \\(2m\\) points.\n\nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) there is a unique line of slope\u202f\\(1\\) whose equation is \\(y=x-(m-1)\\); this line contains precisely the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\).  Delete this line from \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\nNow introduce the two new sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell'_{1}&:y=x-m,\\\\[1mm]\n\\ell'_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n*The line \\(\\ell'_{1}\\).*  \nIts equation is \\(y=x-m\\); it contains all points of \\(A_1\\) except the two extremal points \\((1,2m-1)\\) and \\((2m-1,1)\\).  Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(a+b=2m\\) and therefore \\(b=2m-a\\); substituting into \\(y=x-m\\) gives \\(2m-a=a-m\\), i.e. \\(a=m\\).  Hence the only points of \\(A_1\\) lying on \\(\\ell'_{1}\\) are \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\), which have already been removed from \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell'_{1}\\) covers the whole antidiagonal \\(A_1\\) except the two extremal points.\n\n*The line \\(\\ell'_{2}\\).*  \nIts equation can be written as \\(2y=-x+2m+1\\).  For a point \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(a+b=2m+1\\), i.e. \\(b=2m+1-a\\).  Substituting into the equation of \\(\\ell'_{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2(2m+1-a)=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow a=1\\ \\text{or}\\ a=2m-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell'_{2}\\) contains exactly the two extremal points \\((1,2m)\\) and \\((2m,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) with \\(a\\neq1,2m\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n2b=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow b=\\frac{2m+1-a}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer precisely when \\(a\\) is odd.  Hence all points of \\(A_2\\) with odd abscissa lie on \\(\\ell'_{2}\\).  The remaining points of \\(A_2\\) (those with even abscissa) are exactly the points \\((2,2m-1),(4,2m-3),\\dots ,(2m-2,3)\\); each of them belongs to the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) that we removed, and therefore is already covered by the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_{1},\\ell'_{2}\\}\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{2m+1}\\).\n\nBy induction the statement holds for all odd \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Completion of the proof\n\nSummarising the results obtained:\n\n* Inequality (18) shows that for even \\(n\\) one cannot have \\(k=n\\); for odd \\(n\\) the inequality imposes no restriction beyond \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n* Constructions in Sections\u202f2.3,\u202f2.4,\u202f2.5 provide, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n  \\[\n  0\\le k\\le n-2,\n  \\]\n\n  a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n* Sections\u202f2.4 and\u202f2.5 give families with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines for all even and odd \\(n\\), respectively.\n\n* Section\u202f2.6 supplies a family with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\).\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are realisable, and for even \\(n\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) are realisable while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThis completes the determination of all admissible values of \\(k\\). \u220e"
