"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every odd integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one can realise **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\le n\\); for every even integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) one can realise all \\(k\\le n-1\\) but the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of three logically independent parts.\n\n1. **A parity\u2011based impossibility for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   For a sunny line the slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently the difference \\(a-b\\) of the coordinates of any two lattice points on the same sunny line is constant.  \n   In the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   the values of \\(a-b\\) run from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\).  \n   When \\(n\\) is even the numbers of points of \\(S_n\\) with even and with odd values of \\(a-b\\) are **different** (the even\u2011\\(a-b\\) class contains one point more).  \n   A sunny line always has a fixed parity of \\(a-b\\); therefore a family of only sunny lines can cover at most the smaller parity class, leaving at least one point uncovered.  Hence a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for even \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Explicit constructions for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   The constructions are divided into four sub\u2011cases.\n\n   * (i) **\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\)).**  \n     Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) given by \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,k-1)\\).  \n     The remaining points are covered by the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1)\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  A short case analysis shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on one of these lines, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * (ii) **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n     Let \\(n=2m\\).  Take the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,n-2)\\).  \n     All points with \\(a<b\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny); the single point \\((n,1)\\) also lies on this line.  Adding \\(x=1\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny members covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n   * (iii) **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n     Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Again use the sunny lines \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,n-2)\\).  \n     All points with \\(a<b\\) satisfy \\(a+b=n\\) and therefore lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n\\).  Adding this line gives a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * (iv) **\\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n     We construct, by induction on odd \\(n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \n     \u2013 *Base case \\(n=3\\).* The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n     \\]\n\n     cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).  \n     \u2013 *Inductive step.* Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n       Replace the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) (which contains exactly the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\)) by the two sunny lines  \n\n       \\[\n       \\ell'_1:\\;y=x-m,\\qquad \n       \\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n       \\]\n\n       One checks that \\(\\ell'_1\\) together with the already present lines covers the whole antidiagonal \\(a+b=2m\\) except its two extremal points, and that \\(\\ell'_2\\) covers those two extremal points as well as the whole antidiagonal \\(a+b=2m+1\\).  Thus the new family  \n\n       \\[\n       \\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n       \\]\n\n       consists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{2m+1}\\).  The induction proceeds, giving a covering for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   The parity argument shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\); for odd \\(n\\) the inductive construction provides a covering with \\(k=n\\).  The explicit families (i)\u2013(iii) realise every other admissible value of \\(k\\).  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated at the beginning of the summary. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nFor an integer \\(d\\) we write  \n\n\\[\nL(d)=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the line \\(y=x-d\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).  Every line \\(y=x-d\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is even and that we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with **all** of them sunny.  \nFor a sunny line the quantity \\(a-b\\) is constant along the line, because the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\).  \nConsequently each sunny line contains only points whose difference \\(a-b\\) has a fixed parity (even or odd).\n\nIn \\(S_n\\) the possible values of \\(a-b\\) range from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\).  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even the numbers of points of \\(S_n\\) with even and with odd \\(a-b\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b\\equiv0\\pmod2\\}= \\frac{n(n+2)}4,\\qquad\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b\\equiv1\\pmod2\\}= \\frac{n^2}4 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The formulas follow from a simple counting of the lattice rows; the even\u2011difference class contains one point more.)  \nThus the even\u2011difference class is larger by exactly one point.\n\nA sunny line can cover points of **only one** parity class.  If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of points they can cover is at most the size of the smaller parity class, i.e. \\(\\frac{n^2}{4}\\).  But \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^2}{4}\\) for every even \\(n\\ge2\\).  Hence at least one point of \\(S_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore **no covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\) is even**.  This proves the necessary restriction\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for even }n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\) the two parity classes have the same cardinality, so the parity argument does not forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is of slope\u202f\\(1\\) and therefore sunny; \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(L(i)\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nConsider the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (8) are horizontal, the line \\(d\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n1. If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n2. If \\(b=n-k\\) then \\(a\\le k+1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Hence \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n3. If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have either \\(a+b=n+1\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(d\\)) or \\(a+b=n\\).  In the latter case \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and again \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (7)\u2013(9).  \nThe family consists of \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines (7) being sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\nThe points not yet covered are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n,1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a<b\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(a<b\\) have first coordinate equal to \\(1\\); indeed, if \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then \\(a=1\\).  Hence they all lie on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  Adding \\(v\\) to the family (10) yields a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and the union of the lines is \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nAgain use the sunny lines (10) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).  \nThe uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n,1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a<b\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then \\(a+b=n\\); consequently all such points satisfy \\(x+y=n\\).  Hence they lie on the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\nd':\\;x+y=n,\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  Adding \\(d'\\) to the sunny family gives a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWe prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the point sets  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\\;\n\\{(3,1),(1,2)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich together are exactly the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some odd \\(n=2m-1\\;(m\\ge2)\\) there exists a covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m-1}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{2m-1}\\}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nby \\(2m-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nThe set \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by adding the two outer antidiagonals  \n\n\\[\nA_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m\\},\\qquad\nA_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m+1\\}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nBoth contain \\(2m\\) points.\n\nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f\\(1\\) whose equation is \\(y=x-(m-1)\\); it contains precisely the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\).  Delete this line.\n\nIntroduce the two new sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_1:\\;y=x-m,\\qquad\n\\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \nEvery point of \\(A_1\\) satisfies \\(b=2m-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives \\(2m-a=a-m\\), i.e. \\(a=m\\).  Thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) (the points removed from \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\)) and no other point of \\(A_1\\).  Consequently the remaining points of \\(A_1\\) are already covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2m+1-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2(2m+1-a)=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow a\\in\\{1,2m-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains exactly the two extremal points \\((1,2m)\\) and \\((2m,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  The other points of \\(A_2\\) have odd abscissa; each such point lies on the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) that we removed, and therefore is already covered by the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{2m+1}\\).\n\nBy induction, for every odd \\(n\\) there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Conclusion\n\n*Parity argument* (Section\u202f2.2) shows that a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\) is even, while no parity obstruction exists for odd \\(n\\).\n\n*Explicit constructions* (Sections\u202f2.3\u20132.6) provide, for every admissible value of \\(k\\),\n\n* a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\qquad\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
