            "content": "Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every odd integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one can realise **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\le n\\); for every even integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) one can realise all \\(k\\le n-1\\) but the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of three logically independent parts.\n\n1. **A parity\u2011based impossibility for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   For a sunny line the slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently the difference \\(a-b\\) of the coordinates of any two lattice points on the same sunny line is constant.  \n   In the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   the values of \\(a-b\\) run from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\).  \n   When \\(n\\) is even the numbers of points of \\(S_n\\) with even and with odd values of \\(a-b\\) are **different** (the even\u2011\\(a-b\\) class contains one point more).  \n   A sunny line always has a fixed parity of \\(a-b\\); therefore a family of only sunny lines can cover at most the smaller parity class, leaving at least one point uncovered.  Hence a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for even \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Explicit constructions for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   The constructions are divided into four sub\u2011cases.\n\n   * (i) **\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\)).**  \n     Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) given by \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,k-1)\\).  \n     The remaining points are covered by the \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1)\\) together with the