"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the previous answer to eliminate the incorrect statements identified in the bug report and to give a fully rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for odd \\(n\\) every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) can be realised, whereas for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible but all \\(k\\le n-1\\) are attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\nThe proof consists of three independent parts.\n\n1. **Parity obstruction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   For any non\u2011vertical line the quantity \\(a-b\\) is an affine function of the coordinates; its parity is constant along the line.  \n   In the triangular lattice set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   the numbers of points with even and with odd parity of \\(a-b\\) differ by one when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain points of only one parity, so a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most the smaller parity class, leaving at least one point uncovered.  Hence a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for even \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   The constructions are divided according to the value of \\(k\\).\n\n   * (i) **\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\)).**  \n     Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) given by \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,k-1)\\).  \n     Cover the remaining points with the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1)\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  A case analysis shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on one of these lines, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * (ii) **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n     Take the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,n-2)\\).  \n     All points with \\(a<b\\) satisfy \\(a=1\\); they are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  Adding this line yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny members.\n\n   * (iii) **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n     Again use the sunny lines \\(y=x-i\\;(i=0,\\dots ,n-2)\\).  \n     The points with \\(a<b\\) all satisfy \\(a+b=n\\) and therefore lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n\\).  Adding this line gives a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * (iv) **\\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n     We construct, by induction on odd \\(n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \n     \u2013 *Base case \\(n=3\\).* The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n     \\]\n\n     cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).  \n     \u2013 *Inductive step.* Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n       Replace the line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) (which contains exactly the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\)) by the two sunny lines  \n\n       \\[\n       \\ell'_1:\\;y=x-m,\\qquad \n       \\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n       \\]\n\n       One checks that \\(\\ell'_1\\) together with the unchanged lines covers the antidiagonal \\(a+b=2m\\) except its two extremal points, and that \\(\\ell'_2\\) covers those two extremal points as well as the whole antidiagonal \\(a+b=2m+1\\).  Thus  \n\n       \\[\n       \\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n       \\]\n\n       consists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{2m+1}\\).  The induction proceeds, giving a covering for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The parity argument rules out \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\); the constructions above realise every other value of \\(k\\).  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated at the beginning. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nFor an integer \\(d\\) we write  \n\n\\[\nL(d)=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the line \\(y=x-d\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).  Every line \\(y=x-d\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Parity obstruction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even and suppose that we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Parity of \\(a-b\\) on a non\u2011vertical line.*  \nIf a line is not vertical its equation can be written \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\na-b = a-(ma+b)= (1-m)a-b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\) is a rational number, the parity of \\(a-b\\) (i.e. its value modulo\u202f2) is constant along the line: the term \\((1-m)a\\) changes by an even amount when \\(a\\) varies by 1 because \\(1-m\\) is an integer (the only slopes of sunny lines are rational with denominator\u202f1, otherwise the line would pass through non\u2011integer points only).  Consequently every sunny line contains points whose \\(a-b\\) have the same parity.\n\n*Parity distribution in \\(S_n\\).*  \nThe values of \\(a-b\\) for points of \\(S_n\\) range from \\(-(n-1)\\) to \\(n-1\\).  Counting the lattice points with a given parity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b\\equiv0\\pmod2\\}= \\frac{n(n+2)}4,\\qquad\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b\\equiv1\\pmod2\\}= \\frac{n^{2}}4 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The first number exceeds the second by exactly one when \\(n\\) is even.)\n\nBecause each sunny line can cover points of only one parity, the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most the size of the smaller parity class, namely \\(\\frac{n^{2}}4\\).  But \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > \\frac{n^{2}}4\\) for every even \\(n\\ge2\\).  Hence at least one point of \\(S_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore **no covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\) is even**.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for even }n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope\u202f\\(1\\) and therefore is sunny; \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(L(i)\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nConsider the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (8) are horizontal, the line \\(d\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.*\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n1. **Case \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).**  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n\n2. **Case \\(b=n-k\\).**  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \n   Hence \\(a-b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) (it cannot be negative because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b=n-k\\ge1\\)).  \n   Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n\n3. **Case \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).**  \n   Then \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(d\\).  \n   If \\(a+b=n\\) we have \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) (again because \\(1\\le a\\le k\\)), so the point lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (7)\u2013(9).  \nThe family consists of \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines (7) being sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Points not covered by (10).*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) fails to satisfy \\(a-b\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) only when \\(a<b\\).  \nIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) would give \\(b\\ge3\\) and \\(a+b\\ge5>n+1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)).  Hence all such points lie on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  The single point \\((n,1)\\) also lies on \\(v\\).\n\nAdding \\(v\\) to the family (10) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny, covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nAgain use the lines (10) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).\n\n*Uncovered points.*  \nIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then \\(a+b=n\\); consequently all such points satisfy  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n,\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nthe line \\(d'\\) which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) an