            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The set to be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nConsider the map  \n\n\\[\n\\chi:\\mathbb Z^2\\longrightarrow\\{0,1,2\\},\\qquad \n\\chi(a,b)=(a+b)\\bmod 3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nIf a line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) then \\(\\chi\\) is constant on the set of its integer points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the line be given by \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\).  \nWrite \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line have abscissae of the form \\(a=a_0+q t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)), and the corresponding ordinates are \\(b=b_0-p t\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\na+b = (a_0+b_0)+(q-p)t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(q-p\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions), the term \\((q-p)t\\) is always divisible by \\(3\\); therefore \\((a+b)\\bmod 3\\) does not change when \\(t\\) varies. \u220e\n\nThus every sunny line lies entirely inside one of the three colour classes  \n\n\\[\nS_n^{(r)}=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid \\chi(a,b)=r\\},\\qquad r=0,1,2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA short enumeration of the points of the triangle shows that, for even \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n^{(0)}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}6+\\frac{n}{2},\\qquad\n|S_n^{(1)}|=|S_n^{(2)}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence the two smaller colour classes have size  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf we had a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines, each line would belong to one colour class, so at most \\(n\\) lines could cover at most \\(n\\) points of any given colour class.  Consequently the total number of points that could be covered would be at most \\(n\\cdot M\\).\n\nBut\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot M = n\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr)\n          = \\frac{n^2(n+1)}6-\\frac{n^2}{2}\n          < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |S_n|\n\\]\n\nfor every even \\(n\\ge2\\).  Therefore a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.  This proves that for even \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nHorizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (11) are horizontal, and \\(d\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n1. If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(h_b\\).\n2. If \\(b=n-k\\) then \\(a\\le k+1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n   Hence \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n3. If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(d\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(a+b=n\\) then \\(a\\le k\\) and again \\(a-b\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), so the point lies on \\(\\ell_{a-b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (10)\u2013(12).  \nThe family consists of \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines (10) being sunny.  Hence the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Points not covered by the \\(\\ell_i\\).*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) fails to satisfy \\(a-b\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) precisely when \\(a<b\\).  \nIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) would give \\(b\\ge a+1\\ge3\\) and \\(a+b\\ge5>2m+1\\) for \\(m\\ge2\\)).  \nConsequently all uncovered points lie on the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny.\n\nAdding \\(v\\) to the set (13) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny, covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nAgain use the lines (13) with \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n*Uncovered points.*  \nIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\) then \\(a+b=n\\); indeed \\(a+b\\) cannot be \\(n+1\\) because that would give \\(a-b\\ge0\\).  \nHence every uncovered point satisfies  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n,\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nthe line \\(d'\\) which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus the family consisting of the sunny lines (13) together with \\(d'\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nWe prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(3,1),(1,2)\\}\\); together they cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists (\\(m\\ge2\\)).  \nThe set \\(S_{2m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(S_{2m-1}\\) by adding the two outer antidiagonals  \n\n\\[\nA_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m\\},\\qquad\nA_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2m+1\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f\\(1\\), namely \\(y=x-(m-1)\\); it contains precisely the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) of \\(S_{2m-1}\\).  \nRemove this line and insert the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_1:\\;y=x-m,\\qquad\n\\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2m+1}{2}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \nEvery point of \\(A_1\\) satisfies \\(b=2m-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives \\(2m-a=a-m\\), i.e. \\(a=m\\).  Thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the two points \\((m,m)\\) and \\((m+1,m-1)\\) that were removed; all other points of \\(A_1\\) already belong to the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2m+1-a\\).  Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2(2m+1-a)=-a+2m+1\\Longleftrightarrow a\\in\\{1,2m\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two extremal points \\((1,2m)\\) and \\((2m,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(A_2\\) have odd abscissa; each such point lies on the removed line \\(y=x-(m-1)\\) and therefore is already covered by the other lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\setminus\\{y=x-(m-1)\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{2m+1}\\).  \nThe induction proceeds, establishing the existence of a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Final conclusion\n\n- For even \\(n\\) the three\u2011colouring argument shows that a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible; therefore \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n- The constructions in Sections\u202f2.3\u20132.6 realise every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and also \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
