ion for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\n- **Setup:** \\(n=2m\\). Sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n\n- **Claim:** \u201cA point \\((a,b)\\) fails to satisfy \\(a-b\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) precisely when \\(a<b\\). If \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\). Consequently all uncovered points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\u201d\n\n- **Issue:** **Critical Error**.  \n  - Counterexample for \\(n=4\\) (\\(m=2\\)): the point \\((2,3)\\) satisfies \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b=5\\le5\\) but \\(a\\neq1\\). Hence uncovered points are not confined to \\(x=1\\).  \n  - Because the uncovered set is larger, adding only the line \\(x=1\\) does **not** cover all points of \\(S_n\\). For \\(n=4\\) the points \\((2,3)\\) and \\((4,1)\\) remain uncovered.  \n\n- **Result:** The construction for \\(k=n-1\\) with even \\(n\\) fails.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\n- **Setup:** \\(n=2m+1\\). Sunny lines as before, claim that uncovered points satisfy \\(x+y=n\\).\n\n- **Claim:** \u201cIf \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+2\\) then \\(a+b=n\\); consequently every uncovered point lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\).\u201d\n\n- **Issue:** **Critical Error**.  \n  - Counterexample for \\(n=5\\) (\\(m=2\\)): the point \\((1,2)\\) has \\(a<b\\) and \\(a+b=3\\le6\\) but \\(a+b\\neq5\\). Hence it is uncovered and does **not** lie on \\(x+y=5\\).  \n  - Therefore the uncovered set is not a single non\u2011sunny line, and the proposed covering is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\n- **Base case \\(n=3\\):** The three sunny lines listed indeed cover \\(S_3\\). This part is correct.\n\n- **Inductive step (from \\(2m-1\\) to \\(2m+1\\)):**  \n\n  1. **Assumption:** \u201cAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1, namely \\(y=x-(m-1)\\).\u201d  \n     - **Issue:** **Justification Gap / Critical Error** \u2013 a covering of \\(S_{2m-1