"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have provided a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words: for odd \\(n\\) every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) can be realised, whereas for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible but all \\(k\\le n-1\\) are attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of the lines must be *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   *Lemma 2.1.* On any sunny line the value of \\((a-b)\\bmod 3\\) is constant for all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) lying on that line.  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   A sunny line has a direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) satisfying \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Moving from one lattice point of the line to the next adds \\((q,p)\\) to the coordinates, so the difference \\(a-b\\) changes by \\(q-p\\).  \n   Since \\(p\\neq -q\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), one checks that \\(q-p\\equiv0\\pmod 3\\); indeed the three forbidden directions correspond to the residues \\(0,\\,\\pm1\\) of \\(q-p\\) modulo\u202f3, and the only remaining residue is \\(0\\).  \n   Hence \\(a-b\\) is unchanged modulo\u202f3 along the whole line. \u220e\n\n   Colour the points of \\(S_n\\) by the residue of \\((a-b)\\) modulo\u202f3.  \n   Counting shows that when \\(n\\) is even the three colour classes have sizes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |C_{\\max}| &= \\frac{n(n+1)}6+\\frac{n}{2},\\\\\n   |C_{\\min}| &= \\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(C_{\\max}\\) is the largest class and the other two are equal to \\(C_{\\min}\\).  \n   Because a sunny line can meet only one colour class, at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot|C_{\\min}|\\) points of the smallest class.  \n   Using (2),\n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot|C_{\\min}|=n\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr)\n   =\\frac{n^2(n+1)}6-\\frac{n^2}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   so the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   Consequently for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n3. **Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Four families of explicit line sets are given.\n\n   *Family A (any \\(n\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).*  \n   - Sunny lines: \\(\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\).  \n   - Non\u2011sunny lines: the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\) and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n   In the latter case we have \\(a\\le k+1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); then \\(a-b\\) equals one of \\(-i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   The lines are distinct and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Family B (\\(k=n-1\\), even \\(n\\)).*  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   Points of \\(S_n\\) not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) must satisfy \\(a<b\\).  \n   For even \\(n\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\); thus all uncovered points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Adding this line yields \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Family C (\\(k=n-1\\), odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   Again take \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The points with \\(a<b\\) now satisfy \\(a+b=n\\); they all lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   Adding this line gives a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Family D (\\(k=n\\), odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   We construct, by induction on odd \\(n\\), a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   \u2013 **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n     \\ell_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n     \\]\n\n     contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   \u2013 **Inductive step.**  \n     Assume for some odd \\(m\\ge3\\) we have a set \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_m\\).  \n     Write \\(m=2r-1\\) with \\(r\\ge2\\).  \n     In \\(S_{m+2}=S_{2r+1}\\) the two new antidiagonals are  \n\n     \\[\n     A_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\n     A_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1, namely  \n     \\(\\ell^{*}: y=x-(r-1)\\); it contains precisely the two points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  \n     Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert the two sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell'_1:\\;y=x-r,\\qquad\n     \\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2r+1}{2}.\n     \\]\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \n     Points of \\(A_1\\) satisfy \\(b=2r-a\\).  \n     Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives \\(2r-a=a-r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the two points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) that were removed, while every other point of \\(A_1\\) already lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \n     For \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  \n     Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields  \n\n     \\[\n     2(2r+1-a)=-a+2r+1\\Longleftrightarrow a\\in\\{1,2r\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two ext