 the coordinates, so  \n\n\\[\n(a-b)\\mapsto (a+q)-(b+p)=(a-b)+(q-p).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), one checks that \\(q-p\\equiv0\\pmod 3\\); indeed the three forbidden directions correspond to the residues \\(\\pm1\\) of \\(q-p\\) modulo\u202f3, leaving only residue\u202f0 for sunny directions.  \nThus \\((a-b)\\) does not change modulo\u202f3 along the line. \u220e\n\nNow count the three colour classes in \\(S_n\\).  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even).  The points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the antidiagonals \\(a+b=t\\) for \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,2m+1\\).  \nOn the antidiagonal \\(t\\) the values of \\(\\chi\\) run cyclically \\(0,1,2,\\dots\\); consequently the numbers of points of each colour are\n\n\\[\n|C_{\\max}|=\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}6+\\frac{2m}{2},\n\\qquad\n|C_{\\min}|=\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}6-\\frac{2m}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The class \\(C_{\\max}\\) occurs on the antidiagonals with even \\(t\\), the other two classes are equal.)\n\nA sunny line can meet only one colour class by Lemma\u202f2.1.  Hence at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot|C_{\\min}|=2m\\Bigl(\\frac{2m(2m+1)}6-\\frac{2m}{2}\\Bigr)\n=\\frac{4m^{2}(2m+1)}6-2m^{2}\n<\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}2=|S_{2m}|.\n\\]\n\nThus the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for even }n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for every admissible \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Sunny lines: } &\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\\\\\n\\text{Non\u2011sunny lines: } &h_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\n&d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct because their slopes are different.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \nHence \\(a-b\\in\\{0,-1,\\dots ,-(k-1)\\}\\), i.e. \\(b=a+i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nFinally, the points with \\(b=n+1-a\\) (the antidiagonal) are covered by the line \\(d\\).  \nTherefore every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines in (5).  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nTake the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) then \\(a<b\\).  \nFor even \\(n=2m\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le 2m+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\); thus all uncovered points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \nAdding this line yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nAgain use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \nNow the points with \\(a<b\\) satisfy \\(a+b=n\\) and therefore lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny.  \nAdding this line gives a covering with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\]\n\ncontain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by \\(2r-1\\) sunny lines exists (\\(r\\ge2\\)).  \nIn \\(S_{2r+1}\\) the new antidiagonals are  \n\n\\[\nA_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\nA_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{*}: y=x-(r-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich contains the two points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  \nRemove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_1:\\;y=x-r,\\qquad\n\\ell'_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2r+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\) are sunny (slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \n\n*Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(b=2r-a\\).  \nSubstituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives \\(2r-a=a-r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the two points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(A_1\\) already lie on lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  \nPlugging into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2(2r+1-a)=-a+2r+1\\Longleftrightarrow a\\in\\{1,2r\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the extremal points \\((1,2r)\\) and \\((2r,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  \nEvery remaining point of \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) (slope\u202f1), so it is already covered by the unchanged lines.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2r+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n\\]\n\nis a set of \\(2r+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{2r+1}\\).  \nThe induction proceeds, establishing the existence of a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Synthesis\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 we derived the impossibility (4) for even \\(n\\).  \nConstructions in Sections\u202f2.2.1\u20132.2.3 realise every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n0\\le k\\le n, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\\n0\\le k\\le n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNo other values are possible.  Hence the answer is exactly the set stated at the beginning. \u220e"
