   |C_{\\min}| &= \\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(C_{\\max}\\) is the largest class and the other two are equal to \\(C_{\\min}\\).  \n   Because a sunny line can meet only one colour class, at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot|C_{\\min}|\\) points of the smallest class.  \n   Using (2),\n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot|C_{\\min}|=n\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}6-\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr)\n   =\\frac{n^2(n+1)}6-\\frac{n^2}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   so the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   Consequently for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n3. **Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Four families of explicit line sets are given.\n\n   *Family A (any \\(n\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).*  \n   - Sunny lines: \\(\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\).  \n   - Non\u2011sunny lines: the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\) and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n   In the latter case we have \\(a\\le k+1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); then \\(a-b\\) equals one of \\(-i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   The lines are distinct and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Family B (\\(k=n-1\\), even \\(n\\)).*  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   Points of \\(S_n\\) not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) must satisfy \\(a<b\\).  \n   For even \\(n\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\); thus all uncovered points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Adding this line yields \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Family C (\\(k=n-1\\), odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   Again take \\(\\ell_i: y=x-i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The points