ext adds \\((q,p)\\) to the coordinates, so  \n\n\\[\n(a-b)\\mapsto (a+q)-(b+p)=(a-b)+(q-p).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), one checks that \\(q-p\\equiv0\\pmod 3\\); indeed the three forbidden directions correspond to the residues \\(\\pm1\\) of \\(q-p\\) modulo\u202f3, leaving only residue\u202f0 for sunny directions.  \nThus \\((a-b)\\) does not change modulo\u202f3 along the line. \u220e\n\nNow count the three colour classes in \\(S_n\\).  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even).  The points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the antidiagonals \\(a+b=t\\) for \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,2m+1\\).  \nOn the antidiagonal \\(t\\) the values of \\(\\chi\\) run cyclically \\(0,1,2,\\dots\\); consequently the numbers of points of each colour are\n\n\\[\n|C_{\\max}|=\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}6+\\frac{2m}{2},\n\\qquad\n|C_{\\min}|=\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}6-\\frac{2m}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The class \\(C_{\\max}\\) occurs on the antidiagonals with even \\(t\\), the other two classes are equal.)\n\nA sunny line can meet only one colour class by Lemma\u202f2.1.  Hence at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot|C_{\\min}|=2m\\Bigl(\\frac{2m(2m+1)}6-\\frac{2m}{2}\\Bigr)\n=\\frac{4m^{2}(2m+1)}6-2m^{2}\n<\\frac{(2m)(2m+1)}2=|S_{2m}|.\n\\]\n\nThus the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for even }n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for every admissible \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (any \\(n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Sunny lines: } &\\ell_i:\\;y=x-i\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\\\\\n\\text{Non\u2011sunny lines: } &h_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\n&d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\