an hold only if every sunny line contains exactly \\(n\\) points, i.e. one point\n   from each antidiagonal.  A line that meets every antidiagonal must have slope\n   \\(-1\\) (its intersection with the antidiagonal \\(a+b=t\\) is \\((t-c,c)\\) for a fixed\n   constant \\(c\\)), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence such a covering is\n   impossible.  Therefore for even \\(n\\)\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   *Construction A (works for every \\(n\\) and for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Sunny lines } \\ell_i:\\; y = x - i \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\\\\\n   &\\text{Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines } h_j:\\; y = j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\n   &\\text{Diagonal (non\u2011sunny) line } d:\\; x + y = n+1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The three families have slopes \\(1\\), \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) respectively, so the\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly the sunny lines.  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).  \n   *If \\(b\\ge n-k\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\); hence\n   \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).  \n   Points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by \\(d\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs\n   to at least one line of (2), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Construction B (realises \\(k=n-1\\)).*  \n\n   - **Even \\(n=2m\\).**  \n     Take the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y = x - i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\) (there are\n     \\(n-1\\) of them).  The only points of \\(S_n\\) not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) are those with\n     \\(a=1\\); they all lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  Adding this\n     line yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Odd \\(n\\).**  \n     Again use \\(\\ell_i: y = x - i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  Points not on any of\n     these have \\(a<b\\) and satisfy \\(a+b=n\\); they all lie on the line\n     \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Adding this\n     line gives a covering with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction C (realises \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   We prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct\n   sunny lines.\n\n   - **Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y = x,\\qquad y = -2x+5,\\qquad y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n     \\]\n\n     contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   - **Inductive step.**  \n     Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by \\(2r-1\\) sunny lines\n     exists (\\(r\\ge 2\\)).  In \\(S_{2r+1}\\) the new antidiagonals are  \n\n     \\[\n     A_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\n     A_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1,\n     namely \\(\\ell^{*}: y = x-(r-1)\\); it contains the two points\n     \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert\n\n     \\[\n     \\ell'_1:\\; y = x - r,\\qquad\n     \\ell'_2:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2r+1}{2},\n     \\]\n\n     both sunny.\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \n     For \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(b=2r-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives\n     \\(2r-a = a - r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the\n     two points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  All other points of \\(A_1\\) already lie on\n     the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \n     For \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields\n     \\(2(2r+1-a) = -a + 2r+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\).  Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two\n     extremal points \\((1,2r