            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet a line have primitive integer direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  If the line is sunny then \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 3\\) and the line meets each\nset  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a+b=t\\},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nin at most one lattice point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_t\\) lie on the line, then\n\\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=m(q,p)\\) for some non\u2011zero integer \\(m\\).  Because both points\nsatisfy \\(a+b=t\\), we have \\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=0\\); hence \\(m(q+p)=0\\) and\n\\(q+p=0\\).  Thus \\((q,p)\\) is proportional to \\((1,-1)\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of sunny.  Therefore at most one point of any antidiagonal can belong\nto a sunny line.  Moreover a sunny line cannot have direction \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\)\nor \\((1,-1)\\), so \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for even \\(n\\)\n\nThe antidiagonals intersecting \\(S_n\\) are \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\); there are\nexactly \\(n\\) of them.  By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most one point from\neach \\(D_t\\), hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  The cardinality of\n\\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}|D_t\\cap S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the average number of points per line would be \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Since a\nsunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, equality can hold only if **every**\nsunny line contains exactly \\(n\\) points, i.e. one point from each antidiagonal.\nA line that meets every antidiagonal must have slope \\(-1\\) (its intersection with\n\\(D_t\\) is \\((t-c,c)\\) for a fixed constant \\(c\\)), which is prohibited for a\nsunny line.  Hence such a covering cannot exist.\n\nTherefore, when \\(n\\) is even,\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of lines that realise every integer \\(k\\) allowed\nby (3) (and also the case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction A (all \\(n\\), \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_i &: y = x - i \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\\\\\nh_j &: y = j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\nd &: x + y = n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny; the \\(h_j\\) are horizontal\n(non\u2011sunny) and \\(d\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  All lines are distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\); consequently\n\\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(d\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at\nleast one of the lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction B (realises \\(k=n-1\\))\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \nTake the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i: y = x - i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\) (there are\n\\(n-1\\) of them).  The only points of \\(S_n\\) not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) satisfy\n\\(a=1\\); all such points lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.\nAdding this line yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nAgain use \\(\\ell_i: y = x - i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  Points not on any of\nthem have \\(a<b\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(n\\) odd, must satisfy\n\\(a+b=n\\).  All these points lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\), which is parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Adding this line gives a covering with\n\\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction C (realises \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\))\n\nWe prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny = x,\\qquad y = -2x+5,\\qquad y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n\\]\n\ncontain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by \\(2r-1\\) sunny lines\nexists (\\(r\\ge 2\\)).  In \\(S_{2r+1}\\) the new antidiagonals are  \n\n\\[\nA_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\nA_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAmong the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1,\nnamely \\(\\ell^{*}: y = x-(r-1)\\); it contains the two points\n\\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert\n\n\\[\n\\ell'_1:\\; y = x - r,\\qquad\n\\ell'_2:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2r+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nboth sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(b=2r-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives\n\\(2r-a = a - r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the\ntwo points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  All other points of \\(A_1\\) already lie on\nthe unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields\n\\(2(2r+1-a) = -a + 2r+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\).  Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the\ntwo extremal points \\((1,2r)\\) and \\((2r,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  Every remaining point\nof \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) (slope\u202f1),\nso it is already covered by the unchanged lines.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2r+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2r+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{2r+1}\\).\nThe induction proceeds, establishing a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines for every\nodd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtained the necessary condition (3) for even \\(n\\); no restriction\narises for odd \\(n\\).  Constructions A, B and C show that every integer \\(k\\) obeying\nthe condition can indeed be achieved.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
