"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation** \u2013 Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The required points are exactly the points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is not a multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **A bound for a sunny line** \u2013  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let a line have primitive integer direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \n   If the line is sunny then it meets each antidiagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid x+y=t\\},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n\n   in **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\). Consequently a sunny line contains at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (there are exactly \\(n\\) antidiagonals intersecting\n   \\(S_n\\): \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_t\\cap S_n\\) lie on the line.\n   Their difference is an integer multiple of \\((q,p)\\): \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=m(q,p)\\) with\n   \\(m\\neq0\\).  Because both points satisfy \\(a+b=t\\) we have\n   \\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=0\\), i.e. \\(m(q+p)=0\\).  Hence \\(q+p=0\\) and the direction\n   vector is proportional to \\((1,-1)\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.\n   \u220e\n\n3. **Even \\(n\\): impossibility of \\(k=n\\)**  \n\n   Assume \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at most one point from each antidiagonal,\n   therefore at most \\(n\\) points in total.  The set \\(S_n\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, so the average number of points per line would be \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).\n   Equality can hold only if **every** sunny line contains exactly one point from\n   each antidiagonal, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) points.  A line that meets every\n   antidiagonal must intersect the family \\(\\{D_t\\}_{t=2}^{n+1}\\) in a point for each\n   \\(t\\).  The only lines with this property are those of slope \\(-1\\) (they meet\n   \\(D_t\\) at \\((t-c,c)\\) for a fixed constant \\(c\\)) **or** the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\).  The vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny; the\n   slope \\(-1\\) lines are explicitly excluded from being sunny.  Consequently a\n   covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even, and we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\text{ even}). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit constructions**\n\n   The constructions below work for **all** \\(n\\ge 3\\) and realise every integer\n   \\(k\\) that satisfies the necessary bound (3).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Construction\u202fA (covers all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n\n   Choose integers  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Define three families of lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines*  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x - i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines*  \n   \\[\n   h_j:\\; y = j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *One diagonal (non\u2011sunny) line*  \n   \\[\n   d:\\; x + y = n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct; the \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny,\n   while the \\(h_j\\) are horizontal (\\(0\\) slope) and \\(d\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(b\\ge n-k\\).*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le b-a\\le (n)- (k+1)=n-k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, because \\(b\\ge n-k\\) we have \\(b-a\\le k-1\\).  Consequently\n   \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and the point satisfies \\(b = a-(b-a)\\), i.e.\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \n   Such points are precisely those on the diagonal line \\(d\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and\n   exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.  Hence all integers\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are attainable.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Construction\u202fB (realises \\(k=n-1\\)).**  \n\n   We treat even and odd \\(n\\) separately.\n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \n   Take the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x - i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   (There are \\(n-1\\) of them.)  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not lying on any \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly those with\n   abscissa \\(a=1\\); indeed, if \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\) and\n   \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), so the point belongs to \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n   All points with \\(a=1\\) lie on the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v:\\; x = 1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Adding \\(v\\) we\n   obtain a covering consisting of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \n   Again use the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The points not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) are those with \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a<b\\); they\n   all satisfy \\(a+b=n\\) and therefore lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   d':\\; x + y = n ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny.  Adding \\(d'\\) yields a\n   covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **Construction\u202fC (realises \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).**  \n\n   We prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct\n   sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x,\\qquad y = -2x+5,\\qquad y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n   \\]\n\n   contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by \\(2r-1\\) sunny lines\n   exists (\\(r\\ge 2\\)).  In \\(S_{2r+1}\\) the new antidiagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   A_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\n   A_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope \\(1\\),\n   namely \\(\\ell^{*}: y = x-(r-1)\\); it contains the two points\n   \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell'_1:\\; y = x - r ,\\qquad\n   \\ell'_2:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2r+1}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   both sunny.\n\n   *Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \n   For \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(b=2r-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives\n   \\(2r-a = a - r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the\n   two points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  All other points of \\(A_1\\) already lie on\n   the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n   *Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \n   For \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields\n   \\(2(2r+1-a) = -a + 2r+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\).  Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the\n   two extremal points \\((1,2r)\\) and \\((2r,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  Every remaining point of\n   \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) (slope\u202f1), so it\n   is already covered by the unchanged lines.\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{2r+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(2r+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{2r+1}\\).\n   The induction proceeds, giving a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd\n   \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n\n   * Lemma\u202f1 gives the universal bound \u201cat most one point per antidiagonal\u201d, whence a\n     sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   * For even \\(n\\) the bound together with the observation that a line meeting\n     every antidiagonal must be either slope \\(-1\\) or vertical (both non\u2011sunny) forces\n     \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   * Constructions\u202fA,\u202fB and\u202fC exhibit, for every admissible \\(k\\), a family of\n     \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n     \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence the list of possible values of \\(k\\) stated at the beginning is exact. \u220e"
