e line in parametric form \\((x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+m(q,p)\\) where \\((q,p)\\) is a\nprimitive integer direction vector (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \nAssume two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_t\\cap S_n\\) lie on the line.\nThen \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=m(q,p)\\) for some non\u2011zero integer \\(m\\).  \nSince both points satisfy \\(x+y=t\\),\n\n\\[\n0=(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=m(q+p).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(q+p=0\\), i.e. the direction vector is proportional to \\((1,-1)\\), which\nmeans the line has slope \\(-1\\) and is not sunny \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most one point from each antidiagonal\n\\(D_t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) such antidiagonals.\nHence a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, the average number of\npoints per line is \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Equality can hold only when **every**\nsunny line contains exactly one point from each antidiagonal, i.e. exactly\n\\(n\\) points.  A line intersecting every antidiagonal must satisfy\n\\(q+p=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) or \\(q=0\\) (vertical).  Both possibilities are excluded\nfrom being sunny.  Therefore for even \\(n\\) a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is\nimpossible and we have\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\text{ even}). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\) the above obstruction does not appear, so no a\u2011priori upper\nbound other than \\(k\\le n\\) exists.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions\n\nAll constructions use the same set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction A (realises every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x - i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines*  \n\\[\nh_j:\\; y = j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal (non\u2011sunny) line*  \n\\[\nd:\\; x + y = n+1 .\n\\]\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n1. If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(h_b\\).\n2. If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \n   Hence \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b = a-(b-a)\\),\n   i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n3. If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on \\(d\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and\nexactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.  Hence all integers\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are attainable for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction B (realises \\(k=n-1\\))\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \nSunny lines: \\(\\ell_i: y = x - i\\) for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).  \nThe points not on any \\(\\ell_i\\) are precisely those with \\(a=1\\); they all lie on the vertical line \\(v: x=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  Adding \\(v\\) yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \nUse the same sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\)).  \nThe uncovered points are those with \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a<b\\); they all satisfy\n\\(x+y=n\\), i.e. they lie on the line \\(d': x+y=n\\), which is parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Adding \\(d'\\) gives a covering with\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence \\(k=n-1\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction C (realises \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\))\n\nWe prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines.\n\n*Base \\(n=3