ith \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   - Sunny lines: \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i\\quad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\\)  \n     (These have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny.)  \n   - Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines: \\(\\displaystyle h_j:\\; y = j\\quad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\\)  \n   - Diagonal (non\u2011sunny) line: \\(\\displaystyle d:\\; x + y = n+1.\\)\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   1. If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(h_b\\).  \n   2. If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \n      Hence \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and the point satisfies \\(b = a + (b-a)\\), i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).  \n   3. If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(d\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny. Hence all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are attainable for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction\u202fB (realises \\(k=n-1\\)).*  \n\n   - **Even \\(n=2m\\).**  \n     Sunny lines: \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i\\quad (i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2).\\)  \n     The points of \\(S_n\\) not lying on any \\(\\ell_i\\) are precisely those with\n     \\(a=1\\); they all satisfy \\(x=1\\) and therefore lie on the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     v:\\; x=1,\n     \\]\n\n     which is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  The family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=0}^{n-2}\\cup\\{v\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n   - **Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).**  \n     Use the same sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2\\)).  \n     The uncovered points are those with \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a<b\\); all such points satisfy  \n\n     \\[\n     x+y=n,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. they lie on the line \\(d': x+y=n\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and thus non\u2011sunny.  Adding \\(d'\\) yields a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently \\(k=n-1\\) is achievable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction\u202fC (realises \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   We prove by induction on odd \\(n\\) that \\(S_n\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   - **Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y = x,\\qquad y = -2x+5,\\qquad y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\n     \\]\n\n     contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   - **Inductive step.**  \n     Assume a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by \\(2r-1\\) sunny lines exists (\\(r\\ge2\\)).  \n     In \\(S_{2r+1}\\) the new antidiagonals are  \n\n     \\[\n     A_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r\\},\\qquad\n     A_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2r+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope \\(1\\), namely  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell^{*}:\\; y = x-(r-1),\n     \\]\n\n     which contains the two points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\) of \\(S_{2r-1}\\).  \n     Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert the two sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell'_1:\\; y = x - r ,\\qquad\n     \\ell'_2:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2r+1}{2}.\n     \\]\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_1\\).*  \n     For \\((a,b)\\in A_1\\) we have \\(b=2r-a\\).  Substituting into \\(\\ell'_1\\) gives\n     \\(2r-a = a - r\\), i.e. \\(a=r\\) or \\(a=r+1\\); thus \\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the two points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  All other points of \\(A_1\\) already lie on the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\).\n\n     *Coverage of \\(A_2\\).*  \n     For \\((a,b)\\in A_2\\) we have \\(b=2r+1-a\\).  Inserting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields\n     \\(2(2r+1-a) = -a + 2r+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\).  Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two extremal points \\((1,2r)\\) and \\((2r,1)\\) of \\(A_2\\).  Every remaining point of \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) (slope\u202f1), so it is already covered.\n\n     Therefore  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{2r+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(2r+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{2r+1}\\).  \n     The induction proceeds, giving a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   - Lemma\u202f1 shows a sunny line can meet at most one point from each antidiagonal, hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   - For even \\(n\\) a line meeting all \\(n\\) antidiagonals must be vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny, which yields the upper bound (\u2605).  \n   - Constructions\u202fA,\u202fB and\u202fC exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently the list of possible values of \\(k\\) given at the beginning is exact. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line is sunny then it contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\) from each antidiagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid x+y=t\\},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in parametric form \\((x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+m(q,p)\\) where \\((q,p)\\) is a primitive integer direction vector (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \nAssume two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_t\\cap S_n\\) lie on the line.  \nThen \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=m(q,p)\\) for some non\u2011zero integer \\(m\\).  \nSince both points satisfy \\(x+y=t\\),\n\n\\[\n0=(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=m(q+p).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(q+p=0\\), i.e. the direction vector is proportional to \\((1,-1)\\) and the line has slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis that the line is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for even \\(n\\)\n\nThe antidiagonals intersecting \\(S_n\\) are \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line meets each of the \\(n\\) antidiagonals in **at most** one point, so to cover the \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points we would need every sunny line to meet **every** antidiagonal (otherwise the total number of incidences would be \\(<n\\cdot n\\), which is strictly less than \\(|S_n|\\)). Hence each sunny line contains exactly one point from each antidiagonal, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nA line that meets every antidiagonal \\(D_t\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1)\\) must satisfy either\n\n* \\(x=\\text{constant}\\) (vertical), or\n* slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\nBoth possibilities are excluded from being sunny.  Therefore such a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even, and we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\text{ even}). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\) the above obstruction does not arise, so the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) is the only one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions\n\nAll constructions use the set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction A (all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines*  \n\\[\nh_j:\\; y = j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal (non\u2011sunny) line*  \n\\[\nd:\\; x + y = n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct; the \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) (sunny), the \\(h_j\\) have slope \\(0\\), and \\(d\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nLet \\((a,b