es of \\(\\mathcal L_{2r-1}\\) there is exactly one of slope\u202f1, namely \\(\\ell^{*}: y = x-(r-1)\\).  \n> Remove \\(\\ell^{*}\\) and insert the two sunny lines  \n> \\(\\ell'_1: y = x - r,\\qquad \\ell'_2: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2r+1}{2}\\).  \n> *Coverage of \\(A_1\\).* \u2026 \u201c\\(\\ell'_1\\) contains exactly the two points removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\).\u201d  \n> *Coverage of \\(A_2\\).* \u2026 \u201cSubstituting into \\(\\ell'_2\\) yields \\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\). Hence \\(\\ell'_2\\) contains the two extremal points \u2026 Every remaining point of \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification and Error Identification:**  \n\n1. **Existence of a unique slope\u20111 line** \u2013 The induction hypothesis only guarantees *some* covering of \\(S_{2r-1}\\) by sunny lines; it does **not** force the covering to contain exactly one line of slope\u202f1, nor to be the specific line \\(y=x-(r-1)\\). This is an **Justification Gap**.\n\n2. **Claim about \\(\\ell'_1\\).**  \n   Substituting the coordinates of the two points allegedly removed from \\(\\ell^{*}\\) (the points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((r+1,r-1)\\)) into \\(\\ell'_1: y = x - r\\) gives:\n   * For \\((r,r)\\): \\(r = r - r\\) \u21d2 \\(r=0\\), false for \\(r\\ge1\\).  \n   * For \\((r+1,r-1)\\): \\(r-1 = (r+1) - r\\) \u21d2 \\(r-1 = 1\\), false unless \\(r=2\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell'_1\\) does **not** contain the claimed points. Consequently the replacement of \\(\\ell^{*}\\) by \\(\\ell'_1,\\ell'_2\\) does **not** preserve coverage of the antidiagonal \\(A_1\\). This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n3. **Algebra for \\(A_2\\).**  \n   The substitution into \\(\\ell'_2\\) is mishandled. Starting from \\(\\ell'_2: y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{2r+1}{2}\\) and the condition \\(a+b=2r+1\\) (i.e. \\(b = 2r+1-a\\)), we obtain:\n   \\[\n   2r+1-a = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{2r+1}{2}\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   2(2r+1-a) = -a + (2r+1)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   4r+2-2a = -a + 2r+1\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   2r+1 = a,\n   \\]\n   i.e. **\\(a = 2r+1\\)**, which is impossible because \\(a\\le 2r\\) on the antidiagonal \\(A_2\\). The derivation in the solution (\u201c\\(a\\in\\{1,2r\\}\\)\u201d) is therefore incorrect. Moreover, the statement that \u201cevery remaining point of \\(A_2\\) has odd abscissa and lies on the removed line \\(\\ell^{*}\\)\u201d is unsupported. This constitutes another **Critical Error**.\n\nBecause the inductive construction fails at its core, **Construction\u202fC does not provide a covering with \\(k=n\\)** for odd \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final Conclusion\n\n> \u201cTherefore the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \u2026\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe conclusion relies on the (incorrect) upper bound for even \\(n\\) and on the three constructions, all of which contain critical errors. Hence the final statement is **unsubstantiated**.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly establishes Lemma\u202f1, but the subsequent reasoning that derives the upper bound for even \\(n\\) is mathematically invalid. Moreover, all three explicit constructions (A,\u202fB,\u202fC) contain fatal mistakes: they either fail to cover all required points or rely on unjustified assumptions. Consequently the answer \u201c\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for odd \\(n\\) and \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) for even \\(n\\)\u201d is **not proven**. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
